# Answer 7 · Gendered Inequalities Created by Globalisation (CSS 2019)
@py question 2019-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Accept the premise, define globalisation, then set out the gendered inequalities one by one with evidence, note the counter-evidence, and end with policy.
@ask Globalisation made the world a GLOBAL VILLAGE yet INCREASED INEQUALITIES
@ask DISCUSS the GENDERED INEQUALITIES it has CREATED and PROMOTED worldwide
(1) Introduction: globalisation connects nations but distributes gains unequally, including between women and men
(2) Globalisation and the global village: trade, finance, technology, migration, media
(3) Inequality between nations: core and periphery; women in the periphery bear most costs
(4) Feminised, flexible and informal labour: cheap women's work in export chains
(5) Wage and occupational gaps: women at the bottom of global value chains
(6) Global care chains and migrant domestic workers: care drained from poor to rich
(7) Unsafe work and violence: Rana Plaza, Ali Enterprises, harassment in factories
(8) Austerity and unpaid care: debt conditions shift costs to women
(9) Trafficking and commodification: bodies and beauty sold globally
(10) The digital divide and cultural backlash
(11) Counter-evidence: new jobs and independent income for many women
(12) Conclusion: globalisation is gendered; policy decides who gains
:::

## Decoding the question
The question states a **premise** — globalisation connects the world but increases inequality — and asks you to **discuss the gendered inequalities** it has created and promoted. List them clearly, one per heading, each with **evidence**. A top answer also briefly notes the **counter-evidence** (women's gains) and ends with **policy**, so that the discussion is balanced.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Globalisation** is the growing **interdependence** of societies worldwide through trade, investment, finance, technology, migration and culture. It has made the world a "**global village**" in which goods, money, information and people move faster than ever.

#### ii) The argument
But the village is unequal. Globalisation has **created** new gendered inequalities — in work, pay, migration and safety — and **promoted** old ones by drawing on women's cheap and unpaid labour. It has also brought some women new opportunities; the outcome depends on policy and power.

### 2. How globalisation connected the world
Since the 1980s, trade liberalisation, the founding of the **World Trade Organization** (==1995==), global supply chains, the internet and mass migration have linked economies. Multinational companies now design products in one country, make them in another and sell them in a third. Phones, social media and cheap flights connect families across continents. In this sense the "global village" is real — but its benefits and costs are not shared equally between nations, classes, or women and men.

### 3. Inequality between nations
**Immanuel Wallerstein**'s world-system theory (==1974==) divides the world into **core, semi-periphery and periphery**. Gains from globalisation flow mostly to the core. Because women in the periphery are the poorest workers, they bear the heaviest costs. When a brand shifts orders to a cheaper country, the women who stitched its clothes lose their jobs first, with no severance or social security. The World Economic Forum estimated in ==2026== that the global gender gap was only ==69.2%== closed, with parity ==120 years== away.

### 4. Feminised, flexible and informal labour

#### i) The feminisation of labour
**Guy Standing** (==1989==) showed that global competition made jobs **flexible, insecure and low-paid** — and increasingly filled by women.

#### ii) Export processing zones
From Mexico's *maquiladoras* to Bangladesh's garment zones, employers prefer young women, seen as **cheap and docile**; unions are often discouraged and hours are long.

#### iii) Informal subcontracting
Brands subcontract to **home-based workers** who stitch or embroider on piece rates without contracts or social security — the **"housewifisation"** described by **Maria Mies** (==1986==).

### 5. Wage and occupational gaps
Women are concentrated in the **lowest-paid segments** of global value chains — assembly, sewing, packing, picking — while design, management and profits stay with men and with firms in the core. Pay gaps persist even where women's education has risen. Because women are also expected to do most unpaid care, they are more likely to take part-time, home-based or informal work — which pays less and offers no pension or protection.

### 6. Global care chains

#### i) The chain
**Arlie Hochschild** (==2000==) described how women from the Philippines, Indonesia, Sri Lanka and elsewhere migrate to care for families in rich countries, leaving their own children with relatives. Care is drained from poor to rich households. The migrant earns more than she could at home, but her own children and elders lose her care, and the burden passes to grandmothers and older daughters.

#### ii) Abuse
Under sponsorship (*kafala*) systems in the Gulf, many migrant domestic workers face confiscated passports, unpaid wages and abuse, with little legal protection.

### 7. Unsafe work and violence

#### i) Disasters
The **Rana Plaza** collapse in Bangladesh (==24 April 2013==) killed more than 1,100 garment workers, most of them women. The **Ali Enterprises** fire in Karachi (==2012==) killed about 260.

#### ii) Harassment
Labour groups report frequent verbal and sexual harassment by supervisors in export factories — a **workplace patriarchy** added to the family's.

### 8. Austerity and unpaid care
Global finance and debt brought **structural adjustment** and austerity. **Diane Elson** (==1991==) showed that spending cuts treat women's unpaid labour as "infinitely elastic": when clinics charge fees or subsidies end, women absorb the extra work of caring and stretching food, and girls are often withdrawn from school to help at home. Global finance thus transfers costs from states to women's unpaid time.

::: fig cycle | How globalisation deepens gendered inequality.
@centre Gendered inequality
Cheap feminised labour in export chains
Informal home-based work without rights
Care drained through global care chains
Unsafe factories and workplace harassment
Austerity shifts costs to unpaid care
Trafficking and commodified bodies
:::

### 9. Trafficking and commodification
Open borders and global demand fuel **trafficking** of women and girls. Global beauty and consumer industries sell standards such as **skin-lightening** products across Asia and Africa, turning women's bodies into markets. Commercial surrogacy and the global sex industry also treat poor women's bodies as resources for richer buyers.

### 10. The digital divide and backlash

#### i) Digital divide
Women in poor countries have less access to phones and the internet, so the digital economy's gains flow more to men — Pakistan's mobile-internet gender gap is still ==25%==. Online work and mobile banking, which could help women most, reach them least.

#### ii) Backlash
Rapid cultural change can provoke **conservative reaction** that tightens control over women's dress, mobility and work. In this way globalisation can strengthen local patriarchy even while spreading ideas of equality.

### 11. The counter-evidence
Globalisation also created **opportunities**. Export jobs gave millions of young women in Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and elsewhere their **first independent income**; **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that garment work delayed marriage and increased women's bargaining power. Global norms such as **CEDAW** and **SDG 5** give activists leverage. The picture is therefore **mixed**, not simply negative. After Rana Plaza, the **Bangladesh Accord on Fire and Building Safety** (==2013==) showed that global pressure can also improve women workers' conditions.

### 12. Pakistan and conclusion

#### i) Pakistan
Pakistani women work at the bottom of textile chains as **home-based** stitchers and cotton pickers; many manage households while men work abroad; IMF-linked price rises increase their burdens; and female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%==. **Sindh**'s **Home-Based Workers Act 2018** is a first step to protecting women at the end of global chains.

#### ii) Conclusion
The question's premise holds. Globalisation has connected the world, but it has done so on **gendered terms**: it draws on women's cheap and unpaid labour, drains care from poor to rich, and exposes women to unsafe work and new forms of control. It has also opened doors. Whether women gain depends on **labour rights, social protection, safe workplaces, care services and women's own organisation** — national and global policy can make the global village fairer.

## Why this answer scores
- Accepts the question's premise and sets out **ten gendered inequalities**, each with evidence.
- A **cycle figure** shows how the inequalities reinforce each other.
- **Counter-evidence** (Kabeer) keeps the discussion balanced.
- Current data (GGGR 2026, the 25% digital gap) and a Pakistan section apply the analysis.
